<div id="2026-09-02-hdry-parts-worksheet" class="cl-entry">
<h3>2026-09-02 — HDRY parts worksheet (import review + shop-floor traveler)</h3>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>What.</strong> New printable worksheet at <code>/Documentation/hdry_parts_worksheet</code> listing all <b>63 candidate parts</b> found on disk but not yet imported into <code>printing_3d_models</code>, grouped into 8 categories, deduped by geometry. Each row shows proposed name, proposed SKU, measured volume, PLA weight, bounding box, triangle count, source file(s), a <em>Done</em> tick box and a blank <em>Qty</em> column.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Why.</strong> Two uses: (1) <strong>import review</strong> &mdash; confirm what to create before anything is written (no silent mass-create); (2) <strong>shop-floor traveler</strong> &mdash; print it and tick rows as parts come off the printer, so print-farm staff can work the queue against the project. Also stops re-deriving this analysis every session.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Method.</strong> Geometry-based dedupe: two meshes are the same part when they agree on volume (0.1 cm&sup3;), per-axis bounding box (0.5 mm) <em>and</em> triangle count. Volume alone collides (distinct HDRY parts share volumes) and triangle count alone is unreliable (re-exports differ in tessellation). This correctly folds the BambuStudio duplicate exports (<code>__1_</code> vs <code>__1___1_</code>) into single rows while keeping genuinely different parts apart.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Key findings in the data.</strong> Door hardware: 12 rows &mdash; Female/Male hinge L1+R1, Hinge latch L1+R1, Handle, Window Shape. The hinge pairs are L/R versions, each exported twice under different names; geometry-matched to one row each. Wheel kit: the missing support is <code>Foot__1___1_.stl</code> (292 cm&sup3;) and the tire is <code>Gomma.stl</code> (85 cm&sup3;) &mdash; neither was ever imported, which is why the kit has no support structure. <code>Cerchio.stl</code> and <code>Wheel.stl</code> are the identical 47 mm half-wheel (12.4339 cm&sup3;); <code>Wheel_M.stl</code> / <code>Cerchio__1_.stl</code> are a larger 17.69 cm&sup3; variant.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Files.</strong> <code>root/Documentation/hdry_parts_worksheet.tt</code> (the page), <code>hdry_work/worksheet_data.py</code> (regenerates <code>/tmp/hdry_candidates.json</code>). Theme-compliant: uses <code>var(--*)</code> throughout, has PageVersion header, META block, and an <code>@media print</code> rule that hides UI chrome.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Not done.</strong> No rows imported &mdash; worksheet is review-only pending confirmation. Open questions for the user: is one wheel = 2 half-wheels + 1 tire? Is the foot 1 per unit or 4 (one per wheel)? Is <code>Wheel_M</code> a wanted variant or a duplicate? Which FFL01 variant (Sensor vs no_Sensor) is correct?</p>
</div>
